response:
 To write a C++ program that implements the bubble sort algorithm with minimal memory usage and error avoidance, follow the steps below:

1. First, let's define the bubbleSort function:

```cpp
#include <vector>
#include <iostream>

void bubbleSort(std::vector<int>& arr) {
    int n = static_cast<int>(arr.size());
    for (int i = 0; i < n - 1; ++i) {
        for (int j = 0; j < n - i - 1; ++j) {
            if (arr[j] > arr[j+1]) {
                std::swap(arr[j], arr[j+1]);
            }
        }
    }
}
```

This implementation uses a vector instead of an array since C++ does not have built-in support for sorting arrays without dynamic memory allocation. The vector's underlying memory is automatically managed, so we don't need to worry about manual memory management.

2. Now, let's create a function to parse the given JSON data:

```cpp
#include <nlohmann/json.hpp> // https://github.com/nlohmann/json

void parseJsonAndBubbleSort(const std::string& jsonStr) {
    nlohmann::json j = nlohmann::json::parse(jsonStr);
    std::vector<int> arr = j["array"].get<std::vector<int>>();
    bubbleSort(arr);
    // Use sorted arr here
}
```

3. Finally, integrate these functions into your main function:

```cpp
int main() {
    std::string jsonData = R"({"array":[5,2,9,1,5,6]})";
    parseJsonAndBubbleSort(jsonData);
    return 0;
}
```

